---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import { ArrowUpRight } from '@lucide/astro';
import Button from '../../components/shared/Button.astro';
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import LanguageReference from '../../components/shared/LanguageReference.astro';
import { simpleGrammar } from '../../languages/simple/grammar';

const examples = {
  program: `var message = "Hello, world!"
print(message)`,
  variables: `var count = 0
var pending
count = count + 1
var first = 0
var second = 0
first = second = 5
print(count)`,
  conditions: `var score = 72
if score >= 80:
\tprint("High")
elif score >= 50:
\tprint("Pass")
else:
\tprint("Fail")`,
  loops: `for var i = 0 to 6 step 2:
\tprint(i)

var count = 0
while count < 5:
\tcount = count + 1
\tif count == 2:
\t\tcontinue
\tif count == 4:
\t\tbreak
\tprint(count)`,
  counter: `var i = 0
for i = 3 to 0 step -1:
\tprint(i)
print(i)`,
  functions: `def greet(name = "world"):
\treturn "Hello, " + name

print(greet())
print(greet("Ada"))`,
  closures: `def make_counter():
\tvar count = 0
\tdef next():
\t\tcount = count + 1
\t\treturn count
\treturn next

var counter = make_counter()
print(counter())
print(counter())`,
  indexing: `var items = [10, 20, 30]
items[1] = 99
var record = {"name": "Ada"}
record["score"] = items[-1]
print(record["score"])`,
};
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Simple reference - Kindly"
  description="Simple language reference: syntax, values, expressions, and control flow."
  active="simple"
>
  <h1>Simple</h1>
  <p>Simple programs use named variables, function calls, and indented blocks to describe work.</p>
  <Button href="/editor?language=simple" variant="primary">
    Open the editor <ArrowUpRight aria-hidden="true" />
  </Button>
  <nav aria-label="On this page">
    <a href="#source">Source format</a><a href="#variables">Variables</a><a href="#conditions"
      >Conditionals</a
    ><a href="#loops">Loops</a><a href="#functions">Functions</a><a href="#values">Values</a><a
      href="#expressions">Expressions</a
    ><a href="#collections">Indexing</a><a href="#execution">Execution</a>
  </nav>
  <p>
    This program stores text in a variable named <code>message</code> and calls <code>print</code> to
    display it:
  </p>
  <Code code={examples.program} lang={simpleGrammar} theme="github-light" />

  <section aria-labelledby="source">
    <h2 id="source">Source format</h2>
    <p>
      Statements end at a newline. An expression can be used as a statement; its result is
      discarded. Block headers end with a colon and require an indented body. Use tabs for
      indentation; a dedent must match an earlier indentation level. Blank and comment-only lines do
      not affect blocks.
    </p>
    <p>
      A <code>#</code> starts a comment through the end of the line. Expressions can span lines inside
      parentheses, brackets, and braces; indentation within those delimiters may use spaces.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="variables">
    <h2 id="variables">Variables and assignment</h2>
    <p>
      <code>var name = expression</code> creates a variable with the result of an expression. Omitting
      the initializer assigns <code>None</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>name = expression</code> changes the value of an existing variable. Assignment is an expression
      that returns the assigned value; chained assignment is evaluated right to left. Assigning an undeclared
      global raises a runtime error.
    </p>
    <Code code={examples.variables} lang={simpleGrammar} theme="github-light" />
    <p>
      Indexed assignment updates a list or deque element, or inserts or replaces a dictionary entry.
    </p>
    <Code code={examples.indexing} lang={simpleGrammar} theme="github-light" />
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="conditions">
    <h2 id="conditions">Conditionals</h2>
    <p>
      <code>if</code> evaluates a condition. Optional <code>elif</code> branches are checked in order;
      <code>else</code> runs when no earlier condition was truthy.
    </p>
    <Code code={examples.conditions} lang={simpleGrammar} theme="github-light" />
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="loops">
    <h2 id="loops">Loops</h2>
    <p>
      <code>while expression:</code> evaluates its condition before each iteration. <code
        >for var name = start to stop step increment:</code
      > creates a counter for the loop. The stop bound is exclusive; the default step is <code
        >1</code
      >.
    </p>
    <p>
      Start, stop, and step are evaluated once on entry. Positive and negative steps are supported.
      A zero step runs no iterations. For positive steps, iteration continues while the counter is
      less than stop; for negative steps, while it is greater than stop.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>break</code> exits the innermost loop. <code>continue</code> starts its next iteration; a
      numeric loop still increments its counter. Both are invalid outside loops.
    </p>
    <Code code={examples.loops} lang={simpleGrammar} theme="github-light" />
    <p>
      Omit <code>var</code> to update an existing counter. That binding remains available after the loop.
    </p>
    <Code code={examples.counter} lang={simpleGrammar} theme="github-light" />
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="functions">
    <h2 id="functions">Functions</h2>
    <p>
      <code>def name(parameters):</code> declares a function. Arguments are positional and evaluated left
      to right. Parameters may have defaults; required parameters must precede optional ones. A default
      expression is evaluated on each call that omits that argument, and may refer to earlier parameters.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>return expression</code> returns a value. <code>return</code> on its own and reaching the
      end of a function return <code>None</code>. A top-level return stops the program. Calls with
      an incorrect argument count raise a runtime error.
    </p>
    <Code code={examples.functions} lang={simpleGrammar} theme="github-light" />
    <p>
      A function can call itself. It can also define and return another function. In this example,
      the returned function keeps access to <code>count</code>; successive calls print <code>1</code
      > and <code>2</code>.
    </p>
    <Code code={examples.closures} lang={simpleGrammar} theme="github-light" />
  </section>

  <LanguageReference language="simple" />
</DocsLayout>
